const faq = [
  { q: 'How do ravens attract a mate?', a: 'Ravens attract mates through a combination of aerial displays (synchronised soaring, tumbling, diving) and ground-level behaviours including allopreening (mutual feather-preening), billing (bill-touching), food offering, and vocalisation. The male typically initiates displays; the female\'s receptive behaviour — approaching, allowing allopreening, responding with calls — signals interest. Pair formation often begins in winter or early spring, months before the first egg is laid.' },
  { q: 'What is the raven aerial tumbling display?', a: 'During courtship, Common Ravens perform aerial tumbling — a sequence of rapid manoeuvres in which one or both birds roll sideways, invert briefly, dive, and recover. These displays are performed in soaring flight, often at considerable height. Pairs may fly in tight synchrony with wingtips nearly touching. The tumbling display demonstrates the male\'s flight ability and health; females that observe more elaborate displays may preferentially associate with the displaying male. The displays are most intense in the period before nest building begins.' },
  { q: 'When do ravens start courting?', a: 'Courtship activity in Common Ravens begins in late winter — January to March in the Northern Hemisphere, depending on latitude. Ravens are early breeders; nest construction often begins in February and eggs are laid in March. Courtship displays begin weeks or even months before egg-laying, allowing time for pair bond formation, territory establishment, and mate assessment.' },
];
