    <h1>Raven Courtship: Aerial Displays, Allopreening, and Pair Formation</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven courtship combines spectacular aerial displays — synchronised soaring, tumbling, barrel rolls, and diving — with close-contact ground behaviours including allopreening, billing, and food offering. Displays begin in late winter, well before egg-laying, and serve to form pair bonds, assess mate quality, and reinforce established partnerships in long-paired birds.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Aerial Courtship Displays</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The aerial phase of raven courtship is the most visible component. Displays documented in Common Ravens include:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synchronised soaring:</strong> male and female soar together in tight proximity, matching each other's height adjustments and turns. The coordination required for near-contact soaring demonstrates the male's flight precision and may serve as a compatibility assessment — can these two birds fly in synchrony?</li>
      <li><strong>Tumbling and barrel rolls:</strong> one or both birds execute rapid lateral rolls, brief inversions, and steep dives, recovering smoothly. These displays are acrobatically demanding and may function as honest indicators of flight ability and health.</li>
      <li><strong>Mutual diving:</strong> pairs dive together from height in coordinated plunges, pulling out at low altitude. Sometimes performed repeatedly.</li>
      <li><strong>Chase flights:</strong> the male pursues the female in rapid, twisting pursuit — observed particularly early in pair formation before the female has accepted the male's advances.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ground and Perch Displays</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Aerial displays are complemented by behaviours at perch or on the ground:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Allopreening:</strong> mutual preening of head and neck feathers — areas the bird cannot reach alone. Allopreening is a close-contact bonding behaviour; its frequency increases during courtship and continues through the breeding season, maintaining the pair bond.</li>
      <li><strong>Billing:</strong> the male and female touch and cross bills — a highly intimate gesture in corvid social behaviour.</li>
      <li><strong>Food offering (courtship feeding):</strong> the male offers food to the female — sometimes carried in the throat pouch and regurgitated. This may function both as a gift establishing goodwill and as a demonstration of the male's foraging ability.</li>
      <li><strong>Bow display:</strong> the male bows to the female with fluffed plumage, spreading the wing slightly and ruffling the throat hackles — a posture associated with mild courtship and social submission that invites approach.</li>
      <li><strong>Vocalisation:</strong> raven pairs exchange calls during courtship including soft, melodic vocalisations distinct from their typical territorial and alarm calls. These "quiet" calls are associated with close-range pair interactions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Timing and Sequence</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In Common Ravens at mid-latitudes of the Northern Hemisphere:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>December–January:</strong> courtship activity begins; non-breeding floaters visit established territory-holders' areas, and unpaired birds begin advertising.</li>
      <li><strong>January–February:</strong> aerial displays intensify; established pairs renew their bond with allopreening and display flights; new pairs form.</li>
      <li><strong>February–March:</strong> nest building begins — a joint activity in which both birds contribute material and construction. This parallel construction phase continues the pair bond formation process.</li>
      <li><strong>March–April:</strong> egg-laying; full pair bond established.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What We Don't Know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The specific criteria ravens use to assess mate quality — which display components are most important to females is not established from controlled experiments.</li>
      <li>Whether pair formation in non-Common Raven species follows the same pattern — most published data is from <em>C. corax</em>.</li>
      <li>The role of individual vocalisation in mate recognition and attraction.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

